From 704ec74f3fe2853fba74b5987090daea6b58adaf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 01:39:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 43/48] Let supported sites keep playing media in the
 background

Adds Settings > Media > Background media playback, off by default. When it is on, a
supported video site keeps playing after the browser is backgrounded or the screen locks.

Chromium already does the hard part. A page that is playing audio is not treated as
backgrounded by the scheduler, so it is never frozen, and the media notification, lock screen
controls, audio focus and the media foreground service all work already. The only reason
YouTube stops is that its own script pauses playback once the page sees it was hidden.

So this changes what the page is told, not how playback works. A new web preference is
computed per navigation for an allowlist of hosts and read by Document, which then keeps
reporting the document as visible to script and does not fire visibilitychange. Blink itself
still knows the page is hidden, so the background video optimisations still apply.

Brave solves the same problem by injecting JavaScript, which needs a script injector
component upstream does not have: ExecuteJavaScript is restricted to chrome:// and
devtools:// URLs, and an isolated world cannot change what the page sees. Since this project
builds Blink anyway, doing it at the source avoids the injection and its timing races.

Known incomplete: document.hidden is still reported truthfully, because Document::hidden() has
internal callers in canvas font pruning, layout and view transitions that should keep treating
the page as hidden. A page that checks hidden rather than visibilityState can still tell, and
YouTube does, so playback remains inconsistent until the web-exposed attribute is separated
from the internal one.
